CAT = "arrtmriSafety"; SEG = "SAF"
_g = 1.5 * 10000; assert _g == 15000
_mt = 5 / 10; assert _mt == 0.5
Q = [
("Under the ACR Manual on MR Safety, which zone holds the operator console and controls access to the scanner room?",
 "Zone III", ["Zone I", "Zone II", "Zone IV"],
 "Zone III is the MR Controlled Access Area that serves as the safety interface before Zone IV, the scanner room. Zone I is open to the public and Zone II is where screening happens."),
("Which zone is the magnet room itself, where ferromagnetic objects can become lethal projectiles?",
 "Zone IV", ["Zone III", "Zone II", "Zone I"],
 "Zone IV is the scanner room, which includes the projectile area. Strict screening and access control apply at its door."),
("A nurse who works in MRI part time has basic MR safety training. Under the ACR manual, which level is this?",
 "Level 1 MR Personnel", ["Level 2 MR Personnel", "MR Safety Expert", "Non-MR personnel"],
 "Level 1 personnel have passed basic training to work safely in Zone III. Level 2 personnel, usually MR technologists, have broader training and supervise Level 1 staff."),
("Who gives final clearance for a non-MR staff member to enter Zone III after screening?",
 "Level 2 MR Personnel", ["Any Level 1 staff member", "The security guard on duty", "The person's own supervisor"],
 "The ACR manual gives Level 2 MR personnel final authority to admit non-MR personnel into Zone III after written and verbal screening."),
("A patient reports a cardiac device but does not know its model. What is the first step before deciding whether to scan?",
 "Identify the exact device make and model", ["Scan at 1.5 T, which is safe for all devices", "Place the device in the coil's center", "Ask the patient to sign a waiver"],
 "Safety depends on the specific device and its labeling, so the make, model and conditions are identified and checked before any decision. No field strength is safe for every device."),
("On a device label, what does a yellow triangle with MR inside mean?",
 "MR Conditional", ["MR Safe", "MR Unsafe", "MR tested at 3 T only"],
 "The ASTM labels are a green square for MR Safe, a yellow triangle for MR Conditional (safe only under stated conditions) and a red circle with a line for MR Unsafe."),
("A wheelchair label shows a red circle with a line through MR. What may be done with it?",
 "Keep it out of Zone IV", ["Use it if the magnet is ramped to 1.5 T", "Bring it in once the patient is on the table", "Tether it near the bore"],
 "MR Unsafe items pose an unacceptable risk in the MR environment and must not enter Zone IV."),
("Which force twists a ferromagnetic object so that it aligns with the main magnetic field?",
 "Torque (rotational force)", ["Translational force", "Lenz eddy current", "Magnetohydrodynamic force"],
 "Rotational force, or torque, aligns an object's long axis with the field and is strongest at the center of the magnet. Translational force pulls the object toward the magnet."),
("Where is the translational pull on a ferromagnetic object usually greatest?",
 "Near the bore entrance", ["At the exact center of the bore", "At the far wall of Zone III", "Inside the RF shield door seams"],
 "Translational force depends on the spatial gradient of the static field, which is steepest near the bore entrance. At isocenter the field is strong but nearly uniform, so pull is small."),
("On the ECG of a patient inside a high-field magnet, the T wave looks taller than normal. What causes it?",
 "Blood flow in the static field", ["Gradient-induced nerve stimulation", "Heating of the ECG electrodes", "A true heart attack in progress"],
 "Blood, a moving conductor in the static field, induces a voltage that is added to the ECG, mainly during the T wave. It is an artifact, which is why ECG traces in the bore are for gating, not diagnosis."),
("What is specific absorption rate (SAR) a measure of?",
 "RF power absorbed per kilogram", ["Gradient switching speed in tesla per second", "Static field strength measured in gauss", "Acoustic noise measured in decibels"],
 "SAR describes RF power absorbed per kilogram and so the heating risk. dB/dt describes gradient switching and is related to nerve stimulation."),
("A patient's thighs touch each other during a pelvic scan. Why is a pad placed between them?",
 "To break a skin loop that RF can heat", ["To center the bladder in the coil", "To reduce aliasing in the phase direction", "To keep the patient from moving"],
 "Skin-to-skin contact can close a current loop that RF energy heats. Insulating pads also keep skin from touching the bore and coil cables."),
("Which effect of the switching gradient coils causes the loud knocking during scanning?",
 "Gradient coils vibrating in the field", ["Boiling of the liquid helium", "RF pulses striking the patient's eardrum", "Quenching of the shim coil windings"],
 "Rapidly switched currents in the gradient coils sit in the strong static field, so Lorentz forces make the coils vibrate and knock. Hearing protection is given to every patient."),
("A patient feels tingling and twitching in the back during a fast EPI sequence. Which field causes this?",
 "The switching gradient field", ["The static main field", "The RF transmit field", "The earth's magnetic field"],
 "Fast changes in the gradient field (high dB/dt) induce currents in nerves, causing peripheral nerve stimulation. Systems limit dB/dt below the painful level."),
("Under FDA criteria, a whole-body averaged SAR above what value makes a scan a significant-risk study?",
 "4 W/kg over 15 minutes", ["2 W/kg over 6 minutes", "8 W/kg over 1 minute", "1 W/kg over 30 minutes"],
 "The FDA's 2014 significant-risk criteria list whole-body SAR above 4 W/kg averaged over 15 minutes and head SAR above 3.2 W/kg averaged over 10 minutes."),
("Under FDA criteria, above which static field strength is MRI of an adult a significant-risk study?",
 "8 T", ["3 T", "4 T", "1.5 T"],
 "The FDA criteria set 8 T for adults, children and infants over one month, and 4 T for neonates of one month or less."),
("A 1.5 T magnet is how many gauss?",
 "15,000 gauss", ["1,500 gauss", "150 gauss", "150,000 gauss"],
 "One tesla equals 10,000 gauss, so 1.5 T is 15,000 G."),
("During a quench, what is the main danger in the scanner room?",
 "Helium gas displacing the oxygen", ["A sudden rise in the field strength", "Radioactive release from the magnet", "Loss of the RF shield"],
 "A quench boils off liquid helium, which should vent outside. If venting fails, helium fills the room and lowers oxygen, so everyone leaves and the room is ventilated."),
("A patient in the bore goes into cardiac arrest. What does the ACR manual direct?",
 "Take the patient out of Zone IV for CPR", ["Start CPR in the bore at once", "Quench the magnet before giving any care", "Let the code team bring the cart to the bore"],
 "The patient is removed from Zone IV to a magnetically safe area while MR personnel start BLS. Code teams and their equipment must not rush into Zone IV."),
("Which emergency switch shuts down the static magnetic field of a superconducting magnet?",
 "Emergency Magnet Off (quench) button", ["Emergency Power Off button", "Emergency Table Stop button", "Main circuit breaker in the equipment room"],
 "Only the Emergency Magnet Off control ramps the field down by quenching. Emergency Power Off and table stop cut power to systems but leave a superconducting field on."),
]
